  "query_preview": "../Thursday through Tuesday/...Upper level features \ncontinue to show a rather active pattern through the long range \nperiod. We start out with overall zonal W to E flow over much of \nthe region that will gradually transition to a broad upper trough \nstretching from the Russian Far East to NW Canada by the weekend. \nThe transition is set in motion by an upper low moving out of \nRussia late week that eventually anchors itself over SW mainland \nAlaska. The broad trough stays in place into early next week, but \nseveral short waves are evident rotating around the southern edge \nof it.\n\nThe active weather pattern is still apparent at the surface with a\nseries of fronts expected to move through the region late week,\nthrough the weekend, and into early next week. First the low and\nfront that is approaching on Friday that was a new feature in the\nguidance yesterday is still there, but details are still being\nworked out. Guidance has been somewhat split on whether this\nsystem will track S to N farther west and stronger or farther east\nand weaker. The trend so far looks to be toward the latter, but it\nwill still be a wet (up to 1 to 2 inches of rain Fri morning to\nSat morning) and breezy (winds of 15 to 25 kt for the SE gulf and\ninside waters) Friday regardless. \n\nThe weekend is looking interesting as well with a strong broad \narea of low pressure moving into the gulf from the SW and heading \ntoward the northern gulf coast. In addition to the stronger \nonshore SW flow it will bring, it its wake is a shot of cooler air\nthat will be coming in from the west. Forecast convective \nindices are already indicating that Sunday into Monday may be \nrather convective/showery in nature as the cooler air moves in and\nthe potential is there for some thunderstorms across a large area\nof the gulf and outer coast at that time. I have added a broad \narea of slight chance of thunder at that time with the potential \nthat areas along the outer coast could see more frequent \nthunderstorms from the interaction with the terrain. We will keep \nmonitoring these systems as more details emerge.",
